QB.add(41,[{"n":1,"q":"An 8-year-old girl was brought by her mother to the pediatrician with a history of changes in her behavior and appearance. She has become unusually pale, experiences fatigue easily, and has developed an increased frequency of bruising. Investigations reveal the presence of the Philadelphia chromosome. All of the following are risk factors for the condition except?","o":[{"t":"Ataxia telangiectasia"},{"t":"Klinefelter syndrome"},{"t":"Benzene exposure"},{"t":"Down’s syndrome"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) Down’s syndrome"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"A young child presenting with pallor, easy fatigability, increased frequency of bleeding, and presence of Philadelphia chromosome is suggestive of Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia(ALL)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Acute lymphoblastic leukemia is an acute-onset blood and bone marrow malignancy of B and T lymphoblasts characterized by the uncontrolled proliferation of abnormal and immature lymphocytes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"B-cell type is the most common subtype of ALL."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ALL is M/C hematological malignancy seen in pediatric age groups."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Aetiology"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A - Ataxia Telangiectasia (Neurocutaneous disorder)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"B - Bloom syndrome, Benzene exposure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"C - Chemotherapy."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Nitrosoureas and epipodophyllotoxins"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"D - Down syndrome."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"I - Ionising radiation /Infection: HTLV-1"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"F - Fanconi's anemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"K - Klinefelter syndrome."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"N - NF-1."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"P - Philadelphia chromosome"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Translocations: t(4:11), t(1:19), t(8:14)- Seen in both Burkitt's lymphoma and ALL."}]},{"n":2,"q":"For the past few weeks, a 6-year-old has been experiencing fatigue, pallor, and recurrent infections. Her parents also report that she has been experiencing night sweats and loss of appetite. On examination, she appears pale and has multiple petechiae on her skin. A complete blood count reveals a hemoglobin level of 8 g/dL, a white blood cell count of 150,000/µL, and a platelet count of 30,000/µL. Physical examination reveals hepatosplenomegaly. The peripheral smear is given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"ALL"},{"t":"AML"},{"t":"ITP"},{"t":"CML"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The above-mentioned scenario suggests Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical Features of ALL:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Age : 2-8 years (Fast disease progression), adults: > 50 years."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Weight loss, Night sweats"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anemia : Easy fatigability and pallor"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bleeding : Epistaxis, easy bruising."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Leucocytopenia : Recurrent infections"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bone Marrow proliferation : Bone pain, sternal tenderness"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hepatosplenomegaly, Lymphadenopathy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CNS leukemia : Raised ICP, vomiting, and 6th nerve palsy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"T cell tumors : Mediastinal and testicular mass."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Work up of ALL:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"CBC (Option A) is the first investigation performed while suspecting ALL:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hemoglobin (Hb) : Decreased, indicating normocytic normochromic anemia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hyperleukocytosis : TLC >100,000/µL can lead to leukostasis (more common in AML than ALL) with >20% Blasts."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Elderly Presentation : TLC often remains low throughout ALL."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thrombocytopenia may be present."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Peripheral smear:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The smear shows a high percentage of lymphoblasts , which are larger than normal lymphocytes and have a high nucleus-to-cytoplasm ratio. They often have a scant amount of cytoplasm and may exhibit nucleoli."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Bone Marrow aspiration (Option D) shows blasts >20% ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is also used to classify morphological types into L1, L2, and L3 in the FAB classification system."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"FISH- Fluorescent in situ hybridization shows translocation of genes/chromosomes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"t(9:22) - ALL associated with the Philadelphia chromosome"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"t(8:14) - Associated with Burkitt's leukemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"t(4:11)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"t(1:19)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Flow cytometry (Option C):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CD10, also known as common acute lymphoblastic leukemia antigen (CALLA), is a cell surface marker commonly used in the classification and diagnosis of leukemias and lymphomas, particularly acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"B cell type - CD19+, CD79a+."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"T cell type - CD3+"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cytochemistry - TdT+ (Terminal Deoxynucleotidyl Transferase)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Lumbar puncture - CSF should be checked to rule out CNS leukemia."}],"qi":[6743]},{"n":3,"q":"Which of the following statements accurately describes the management phases of acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL)?","o":[{"t":"Induction therapy aims for complete remission using only glucocorticoids and L-asparaginase."},{"t":"Induction therapy primarily focuses on preventing CNS involvement and uses only low-intensity agents."},{"t":"Consolidation therapy for high-risk T-cell ALL includes cyclophosphamide, cytarabine, vincristine, and anthracyclines."},{"t":"Immunotherapy is routinely given during the induction phase to all patients with ALL."}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Consolidation therapy for high-risk T-cell ALL includes cyclophosphamide, cytarabine, vincristine, and anthracyclines."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation :"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The management of acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) includes three phases:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Induction Therapy : Aims for complete remission or even complete remission with Minimal residual disease (MRD) not detectable and restoring normal hematopoiesis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Common regimens include vincristine, glucocorticoids (dexamethasone/prednisolone), L-asparaginase, and daunorubicin . (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Patients with minimal residual disease at the end of induction may receive immunotherapy (e.g., blinatumomab, dasatinib ). (Option D)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"NOTE:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Complete Remission- Leukemic cells not detectable by light microscopy.(Blasts<5% in bone marrow)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Minimal residual disease- Detectable residual leukemic cells via flow cytometry, PCR etc but not detectable via light microscopy."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Complete molecular remission- Patient in complete remission along with no Minimal residual disease."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Consolidation Therapy : Focuses on deepening remission and CNS treatment. (Option B)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Low-risk : Intrathecal methotrexate therapy, vincristine, and 6-Mercaptopurine."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Standard/Intermediate risk : Includes glucocorticoids, cyclophosphamide, cytarabine, vincristine, asparaginase, 6-MP, and methotrexate."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"High-risk/T-cell ALL : Cyclophosphamide, cytarabine, vincristine, asparaginase, 6-MP, high-dose methotrexate, additional glucocorticoids, and anthracyclines."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Maintenance Therapy : Lasts 2-3 years and includes vincristine, 6-MP, prednisolone, and methotrexate."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In Ph-positive ALL , maintenance therapy includes BCR/ABL Tyrosine kinase inhibitors like Imatinib, Dasatinib, Nilotinib, etc are used."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anti CD20- rituximab"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anti CD22- inotuzumab ozogamicin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anti CD19- blinatumomab"}]},{"n":4,"q":"A 7-year-old girl who was diagnosed with ALL complained of severe headaches, experienced nausea and vomiting, diplopia, and developed an unsteady gait a few months after the diagnosis. Which of the following would have prevented this patient's condition?","o":[{"t":"Intrathecal methotrexate"},{"t":"Nilotinib"},{"t":"Blinatumomab"},{"t":"Vincristine"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) Intrathecal methotrexate"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The aforementioned scenario indicates the occurrence of CNS leukemia. CNS ALL is much easier to prevent than treat."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Intrathecal methotrexate is a procedure involving the administration of methotrexate directly into the cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) within the spinal canal."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This method is employed to treat or prevent the spread of cancer cells to the central nervous system (CNS) in ALL cases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Performing a lumbar puncture for CSF cytology carries a risk of Iatrogenic CNS leukemia due to potential bleeding from ruptured epidural vessels, which can introduce blood cells into the CSF. To mitigate this risk, intrathecal methotrexate is often administered during the same session."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Systemic high-dose chemotherapy and cranial radiation therapy are other modalities used to manage CNS involvement in patients with high-risk ALL."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Intrathecal methotrexate, cytarabine, and dexamethasone combination therapy along with cranial irradiation (24 Gy), can also be used."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Nilotinib (Option B): Inhibits the activity of tyrosine kinases, including BCR-ABL, which is characteristic of CML and some forms of ALL. Though TKIs are used in Ph-positive ALL Nilotinib does not cross the blood-brain barrier."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Blinatumomab (Option C): This is used for the treatment of Philadelphia chromosome-positive high-risk ALL."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Vincristine (Option D): It is part of a treatment regimen for low-risk ALL not really used in prophylaxis of CNS ALL."}]},{"n":5,"q":"Which of the following is/are good prognostic markers in patients with ALL? Female gender Leukocyte count >100000/uL. Hyperploidy. L2 ALL.","o":[{"t":"3 only"},{"t":"2 and 3"},{"t":"2,3,4"},{"t":"1,3,4"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Favorable Prognostic factors Unfavorable prognostic factors"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Age: 1-10 years Extreme age group: <1 year or >10 years Leucocyte count < 50000/µl Hypoploidy Hyperploidy t(9:22) and t(4:11) t (12:21) L2 and L3 ALL and mature B cell ALL L1 ALL Total leukocyte count >50,000/cubic.mm Response to treatment Black males Early Pre B-cell ALL Organomegaly Female CNS Leukemia"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Absence of CD10 Remission> 14 days"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Response to treatment is the most important prognostic marker in ALL."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Remission status at 14 days of chemotherapy is the best guide to prognosis."}]},{"n":6,"q":"A 10-year-old girl has been presenting to the emergency department with fatigue, pallor, and recurrent infections over the past month. On examination, she shows hepatosplenomegaly and petechiae. Laboratory tests reveal leukocytosis with a predominance of lymphoblasts, low hemoglobin, and thrombocytopenia. A bone marrow biopsy demonstrates more than 20% lymphoblasts. Cytogenetic analysis shows the presence of the Philadelphia chromosome. Based on the FAB classification, what is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"ALL-L1 (Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia, Small Lymphoblasts)"},{"t":"ALL-L2 (Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia, Large Lymphoblasts)"},{"t":"ALL-L3 (Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia, Burkitt-type)"},{"t":"Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML)"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct answer: C) ALL-L3 (Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia, Burkitt-type)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The French-American-British (FAB) classification divides acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) into three categories based on the morphology of the leukemic cells: ALL-L1: Characterized by small, uniform lymphoblasts with scant cytoplasm. ALL-L2: Involves larger, more pleomorphic lymphoblasts. ALL-L3: Associated with Burkitt-type morphology, which features larger cells with abundant cytoplasm and a prominent nucleolus."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ALL-L1: Characterized by small, uniform lymphoblasts with scant cytoplasm."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ALL-L2: Involves larger, more pleomorphic lymphoblasts."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ALL-L3: Associated with Burkitt-type morphology, which features larger cells with abundant cytoplasm and a prominent nucleolus."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ALL-L1: Characterized by small, uniform lymphoblasts with scant cytoplasm."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ALL-L2: Involves larger, more pleomorphic lymphoblasts."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ALL-L3: Associated with Burkitt-type morphology, which features larger cells with abundant cytoplasm and a prominent nucleolus."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fatigue, pallor, recurrent infections, bone pain, and hepatosplenomegaly, are typical of leukemia due to bone marrow infiltration."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The presence of leukocytosis with a predominance of lymphoblasts, low hemoglobin, and thrombocytopenia supports the diagnosis of acute leukemia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The finding of more than 20% lymphoblasts in the bone marrow confirms an acute leukemic process."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Identifying the Philadelphia chromosome typically indicates a poor prognosis and is more commonly associated with ALL-L3 (Burkitt-type) leukemia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"CYTOLOGIC L1 L2 L3 FEATURES"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cell size Small cells predominate, Large heterogeneous in Large homogenous"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"homogenous size"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cytoplasm Scanty Variable often moderately Moderately"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"abundant abundant"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Nucleoli Small One or more, often large One or more,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"prominent"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Nuclear shape Homogenous Variable heterogenous Stippled homogenou"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"s"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Nuclear shape Regular Irregular clefts regular"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cytoplasmic basophili variable variable Intensely basophilic a"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cytoplasmic vacuolati variable variable prominent on"},{"k":"img","v":6760},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"(A) A noncancerous cell, (B) L1 Type ALL, (C) L2 Type ALL, (D) L3 Type ALL."}]},{"n":7,"q":"Match the following CD markers with the corresponding type of Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL): CD Markers Type of ALL 1. Common ALL a) CD1a 2. Pre B ALL b) CD10 3. Cortical T-ALL c) cylg, CD10 4. T lineage ALL d) CD3\nCD Markers Type of ALL\n1. Common ALL a) CD1a\n2. Pre B ALL b) CD10\n3. Cortical T-ALL c) cylg, CD10\n4. T lineage ALL d) CD3","o":[{"t":"1-a, 2-b, 3-c, 4-d"},{"t":"1-b, 2-c, 3-d, 4-a"},{"t":"1-b, 2-c, 3-a, 4-d"},{"t":"1-a, 2-b, 3-d, 4-a"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) 1-b, 2-c, 3-a, 4-d"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"CD Markers Type of ALL Mutation"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"B-lineage ALL HLA DR, TdT, CD19, CD79, CD22"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pro B-ALL No additional markers t(4;11)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Common ALL CD10 t(9;22)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pre B ALL cylg, CD10 t(1;19)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mature B-ALL CD10, slg t(8;14) t(2;8) t(8;22)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"T lineage ALL CD3, CD7, CD2, CD5"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Early Pro or Pre T-ALL No additional markers t(10;14) t(11;14)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cortical T-ALL CD1a"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mature T-ALL CD4, CD8"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"NOTE:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"B lineage ALL is the most common type."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"T lineage ALL commonly presents with mediastinal mass whereas B lineage ALL involves all organs."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Both mature B ALL and T lineage ALL, more commonly present with CNS involvement."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"WHO classification of Lymphoid neoplasms"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"I. Precursor B-Cell Neoplasms"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia/lymphoma (B-ALL)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"II. Peripheral B-Cell Neoplasms"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Chronic lymphocytic leukemia/small lymphocytic lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"B-cell prolymphocytic leukemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Splenic and nodal marginal zone lymphomas"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Extranodal marginal zone lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mantle cell lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Follicular lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Marginal zone lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hairy cell leukemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Plasmacytoma/plasma cell myeloma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Burkitt lymphoma"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"III. Precursor T-Cell Neoplasms"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia/lymphoma (T-ALL)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"IV. Peripheral T-Cell and NK-Cell Neoplasms"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"T-cell prolymphocytic leukemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Large granular lymphocytic leukemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mycosis fungoides/Sézary syndrome"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Peripheral T-cell lymphoma, unspecified"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anaplastic large-cell lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Adult T-cell leukemia/lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"NK-cell leukemia"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"V. Hodgkin Lymphoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Classic Nodular sclerosis Mixed cellularity Lymphocyte-rich Lymphocyte depletion"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nodular sclerosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mixed cellularity"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lymphocyte-rich"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lymphocyte depletion"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nodular lymphocyte predominant"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nodular sclerosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mixed cellularity"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lymphocyte-rich"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lymphocyte depletion"}]},{"n":8,"q":"A 32-year-old male presents with fatigue, fever, and easy bruising. Blood tests reveal leukocytosis with a high percentage of blast cells. A bone marrow biopsy confirms the diagnosis of Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL). Further genetic testing is performed, revealing the following mutation. What is the likely molecular weight of the BCR-ABL fusion protein associated with the Philadelphia chromosome in this patient's Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia?","o":[{"t":"190 kDa"},{"t":"210 kDa"},{"t":"230 kDa"},{"t":"250 kDa"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) 190 kDa"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The Philadelphia chromosome is defective and unusually short because of reciprocal translocation , t(9;22), of genetic material between chromosome 9 and chromosome 22 , and contains a fusion gene called BCR-ABL1 ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Depending on the location of the breakpoint the molecular weight of the fusion protein varies and has various outcomes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"190kDa - Ph-positive Acute lymphocytic leukemia (ALL)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"210 kDa - Chronic myeloid leukemia (CML)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"230 kDa - Chronic myeloid leukemia with neutrophilia (CML-N)"}],"qi":[6748]},{"n":9,"q":"An 8-year-old girl was brought by her mother to the pediatrician with a history of changes in her behavior and appearance. She has become unusually pale, experiences fatigue easily, and has developed an increased frequency of bruising. Investigations reveal a high WBC count along with high lymphoblast. Even after completion of the chemotherapy treatment regimen, she presents with hematological relapse. Which of the following drugs can be used in this situation?","o":[{"t":"Ponatinib"},{"t":"Methotrexate"},{"t":"Blinatumomab"},{"t":"Cytarabine"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Blinatumomab"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The given clinical scenario indicates that the girl is suffering from Acute Lymphoblastic leukemia. In case of relapse post-chemotherapy newer drugs like Immunotherapy agents or activated T cells can be used."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"B-lineage blast cells express a variety of specific antigens, and monoclonal antibodies against those antigens are used in treating ALL. However, these immunotherapeutic agents are not used in initial therapy for children due to side effects on the CNS, growth, cytokine storm, fever, hypertension etc."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Anti CD20 B- Precursor Rituximab"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Burkitt Leukemia/Lymphoma Ofatumomab"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Anti CD22 B- Precursor Inotuzumab"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Mature B-ALL Epratuzumab"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Moxetumomab Pasudotox"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Anti CD19 B- Precursor Blinatumomab"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Mature B-ALL CAR-T cells"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Note: Blinatumomab is a bi-specific antibody , that combines single-chain antibodies to CD19 and CD3 such that T-cells are brought into proximity with and lyse with the CD19-bearing B-cells."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ponatinib (Option A): A tyrosine kinase inhibitor used specifically for Philadelphia chromosome-positive (Ph+) ALL , not typically first-line for general relapse."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Methotrexate (Option B): A standard part of initial chemotherapy for ALL, but not typically used alone for relapse ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cytarabine (Option D): Primarily used in AML and sometimes in ALL regimens, but not the most targeted option for relapsed ALL ."}]}]);
QB.imgs({"6760":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","6748":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","6743":"data:image/jpeg;base64,"});